    "message": "fix(view): keep \"absent\" and \"unreadable\" apart, and screen the rev at its source\n\nReview of the first commit found three regressions in it, all one mistake:\nreplacing the `existsSync` guards with bare `catch {}` made an unreadable\nfile indistinguishable from a missing one. Both callers of `readAlivePid`\ntreat null as licence to act, so that reads badly:\n\n- `ix view` took an unreadable compass.pid as \"not running\", spawned the\n  detached server, and only then died writing the PID — leaving a visualizer\n  with nothing recording it, which `ix view stop` reports as not running and\n  no later `ix view` can get past. Reproduced: spawn called, then EISDIR.\n- `ix view stop` against a root-owned compass.pid deleted a live server's\n  pid/scope/port (unlink needs the directory permission, not the file's) and\n  then killed nothing.\n- An unreadable compass.scope silently skipped the cross-workspace warning\n  and handed back another workspace's graph — the exact confusion that file\n  exists to prevent.\n\n`readTextIfPresent` now returns null only for ENOENT and rethrows the rest,\nwhich is what `main` did, without the racy check. `readRunningPort` keeps\nits swallow-all policy on purpose and says why: it looks up a URL to print,\nnot liveness. The scope read also stops treating a zero-length file as a\nworkspace named \"\".\n\nThe rev change was directionally right with a false rationale. `currentRev`\nis read only by `ix status` — incremental skipping runs off the mtime map —\nso a rejected rev costs a wrong Revision line, not a re-ingest. Comment and\ntest header corrected. More to the point the guard was at the last stop: the\nvalue enters at the accumulator, where a bare `>` compares it as a string,\nso \"9\" becomes the max and \"10\" > \"9\" is then false and the max walks\nbackwards. `advanceRev` screens it there, which fixes the max, the\n`--format json` contract and the baseline at once. `isRev` is now one\npredicate both sides share — they previously sat three lines apart and\ndisagreed on whether 0 was valid. `previousRev` is looked up lazily; it\nparses the whole baseline, and `onCommitted` saves once per deleted file.\n\nTests: the scope-mismatch branch had no coverage in the whole suite (every\ncase seeded \"*all*\" and launched with --all), and the PID test asserted a\ncleanup that cannot happen. Both replaced, plus the vacuous NaN case, which\npassed against the bug. All five new assertions were checked against\nrestored mutants: swallow-all resurrects the orphan spawn and the silent\nscope warning, and dropping the rev screen lets \"9\" win the max.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
